The appointment of Laith al-Khazali as Iraq’s deputy prime minister came after the Iraqi government appealed to the Trump administration to drop its opposition to the move over his ties to an Iran-backed militia, two Iraqi political sources told MBN. Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi argued that appointing a figure whose group had agreed to give&hellip;
Al-Zaidi conveyed messages to the U.S. government through Tom Barrack, the U.S. special envoy for Iraq and Syria, according to an Iraqi source familiar with the matter in Washington, who is close to the Iraqi government and works to bolster its standing with U.S. policymakers.
The source, who declined to be identified because he was not authorized to speak to the media, said al-Zaidi told U.S. officials that he had to “reward those who have given up their weapons so that I can attract as many factions as possible.”
